<!-- ============ WHY DEEP-DRAWN ============ -->
<section class="section full-width" id="why">
  <div class="section-head reveal">
    <p class="kicker">Why it matters</p>
    <h2>An even shell<br>is a predictable shell.</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="makers-grid">
    <div class="reveal">
      <p>Ayasa's deep-draw tooling keeps the steel's thickness even across the whole form — something hydroforming, spinning, rolling and hand-sinking can't match. Tone fields sit where the tuner expects them, tuning is faster and more precise, and the finished instrument holds its voice for years.</p>
      <p>When a maker chooses Ayasa steel, they're not outsourcing craft — they're starting from the most consistent canvas in the industry. The community has a name for what that earned the company: <strong>the makers' maker</strong>.</p>
      <p>The shop is run by Roy van den Bor, and behind it stands the same workshop that builds Ayasa's own instruments — so every question about annealing, tuning or material behaviour is answered by people who hammer these shells daily.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="maker-materials reveal">
      <figure class="photo-tile">
        <img src="{{ 'makers-top-and-bottom.jpg' | asset_url }}" alt="A deep-drawn top shell and a bottom shell with port, side by side" width="1802" height="850" loading="lazy">
        <figcaption>Top and bottom — deep-drawn to an even 0.9–1.0&nbsp;mm</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="fact"><strong>Top shells</strong><p>The playing side. Every steel, every batch — and convertible to a bottom by forming your own port.</p></div>
      <div class="fact"><strong>Bottom shells</strong><p>Delivered ready: port, neck and a curled collar you can feel with your fingers.</p></div>
      <div class="fact"><strong>Tools &amp; supplies</strong><p>Tuning rings, hammers, magnets, Merbenit, Phoenix Oil, Scotchbrite — the workshop's own kit.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="detail-strip reveal">
    <figure>
      <img src="{{ 'makers-shop-6.jpg' | asset_url }}" alt="Close-up of the curled collar around a shell's port" width="1000" height="672" loading="lazy">
      <figcaption>The curled collar</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <img src="{{ 'makers-shop-7.jpg' | asset_url }}" alt="Close-up of a cleanly formed port on a raw shell" width="1000" height="667" loading="lazy">
      <figcaption>The port, formed clean</figcaption>
    </figure>
